    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i get that officially there's no 'proven' way, but in science we talk about theoretical possibilities all the time. for example, tropospheric scatter or ducting can move signals way further than usual through atmospheric layers. if someone were using advanced localized modulation, wouldn't the density shifts i'm seeing actually make those signals *more* stable or easier to hide in the background noise? like, if you were trying to send something discretely, wouldn't you use those exact atmospheric windows to mask the transmission?",
